WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.800
Mate two yellow mice as often as you like, and you will never get a yellow mouse

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.460
that breeds true. That looked like a failure of Mendel's rules.

3
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.160
It was not. For unlinked genes, segregation and independent assortment keep working

4
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.240
in every case here.

5
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.620
What changes is the map from genotype to phenotype.

6
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:28.053
Cross red snapdragons with white ones, and every F1 flower is pink.

7
00:00:28.913 --> 00:00:30.373
This is incomplete dominance.

8
00:00:31.493 --> 00:00:35.713
Self the pink plants, and red and white come back, at 1 red, 2 pink, 1 white.

9
00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:39.853
Nothing blended, because a pink plant is a heterozygote.

10
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:45.073
In Mendel's peas the 1:2:1 was hidden, but here the colours show it plainly.

11
00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:51.097
Now back to the yellow mouse, whose yellow allele is dominant for coat colour but

12
00:00:51.097 --> 00:00:52.277
recessive for survival.

13
00:00:53.137 --> 00:00:58.297
A mouse with two copies dies before implantation, so 1/4 of the conceptions die.

14
00:00:59.157 --> 00:01:04.157
The survivors are 2 yellow to 1 black, so 12 live pups should be 8 yellow and 4

15
00:01:04.157 --> 00:01:08.397
black. Every live yellow mouse is therefore a heterozygote.

16
00:01:09.137 --> 00:01:10.517
That is why none breeds true.

17
00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:16.397
When two genes act on one trait, a genotype at one can hide the other, which is

18
00:01:16.397 --> 00:01:22.357
called epistasis. In Labrador retrievers, a dominant B makes black pigment, and its

19
00:01:22.357 --> 00:01:24.717
recessive form makes brown, called chocolate.

20
00:01:25.597 --> 00:01:30.977
Cross two black dogs heterozygous at both genes, and the sixteen boxes sort into the

21
00:01:30.977 --> 00:01:31.977
usual four classes.

22
00:01:32.737 --> 00:01:37.637
But a dog with two copies of the recessive form of E has a yellow coat, whatever its

23
00:01:37.637 --> 00:01:43.717
B genotype. So the last two classes merge, and the ratio is 9 black, 3 chocolate, 4

24
00:01:43.717 --> 00:01:44.717
yellow.

25
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:48.170
Other interactions regroup the same sixteen boxes.

26
00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.570
If both genes need a dominant allele to make colour, the ratio is 9 to 7.

27
00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:59.050
If a dominant allele at one gene hides the other, the ratio is 12 to 3 to 1.

28
00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:04.030
If a dominant allele at either gene is enough, the ratio is 15 to 1.

29
00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.630
In every case, only the labels on the boxes change.

30
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:11.193
The boxes never changed, only their labels.

31
00:02:12.113 --> 00:02:14.713
One allele hides the other, and you see 3:1.

32
00:02:15.573 --> 00:02:20.293
Neither hides, you see 1:2:1, and if one class dies, you see 2:1.

33
00:02:21.193 --> 00:02:26.593
Keep this rule. Segregation keeps working in every case here, and only the map from

34
00:02:26.593 --> 00:02:28.313
genotype to phenotype changes.
